---
import ServiceLayout from '../../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import { A } from '../../lib/assets';

const showcase = [
  { asset: A.stampedDriveway, caption: 'Stamped and colored driveway with a scored border, Conejo Valley.' },
  { asset: A.spanishDrive, caption: 'Poured driveway and flagstone walkway to a Spanish-style frontage.' },
  { asset: A.dosVientosDrive, caption: 'Stamped driveway running beside a stone-faced retaining wall, Dos Vientos.' },
  { asset: A.fountainPavers, caption: 'Tiered fountain set into a paver surround with planted beds.' },
  { asset: A.concreteCounter, caption: 'Poured outdoor kitchen counter with a stainless built-in.' },
  { asset: A.polishedCounter, caption: 'Polished concrete counter finished to a wet look.' },
];

const gallery = [
  A.herringboneClose, A.roseWalkway, A.curvedDrive,
  A.paverDrive, A.herringboneWet, A.driveApron, A.potteryEntry,
];
---

<ServiceLayout
  title="Custom & Stamped Concrete"
  eyebrow="Concrete"
  intro="Custom designed and poured concrete driveways, pavers, water features and more — across Newbury Park, Thousand Oaks and the wider Conejo Valley."
  description="Stamped concrete Newbury Park and custom poured concrete across Thousand Oaks and the Conejo Valley: driveways, pavers, patios, countertops and water features. Licensed, insured, guaranteed completion dates."
  headerAsset={A.curvedRoseWalk}
  serviceName="Custom and stamped concrete"
  showcase={showcase}
  gallery={gallery}
  galleryHeading="Flatwork, pavers and finishes"
>
  <h2>Stamped concrete in Newbury Park and Thousand Oaks</h2>
  <p>
    Concrete is the largest single surface most homes have, and it is the one
    people look at every day on the way to the front door. A driveway that cracks
    along the wrong line, or a patio that holds water in the middle, is expensive
    to put right — so the value sits in the sub-base, the reinforcement and the
    joint layout long before anyone sees a finish.
  </p>
  <p>
    CPF designs and pours custom concrete: driveways and aprons, patios, paver
    walkways, poured countertops, and water features. Stamped and colored
    finishes are chosen against the house — the roof tile, the stone, the render —
    rather than off a chart, because a stamp that fights the elevation reads as
    obviously applied.
  </p>

  <h3>What that covers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Driveways and aprons</strong> — formed, reinforced and poured, with jointing set out to control where it cracks.</li>
    <li><strong>Stamped and colored flatwork</strong> — patterns and release colors matched to the house.</li>
    <li><strong>Patios and paver walkways</strong> — laid on a proper base so they stay flat.</li>
    <li><strong>Poured countertops</strong> — indoor and outdoor, sealed for the weather they will actually see.</li>
    <li><strong>Water features and fountains</strong> — formed, plumbed and finished.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Working on a slope</h3>
  <p>
    Much of the Conejo Valley is built on grade. Flatwork on a slope is a drainage
    problem before it is a concrete problem: where the water goes at the bottom of
    a driveway decides whether the slab lasts. Falls, drains and the transition
    into the retaining structure get designed together, not bolted on afterwards.
  </p>

  <h2>Guaranteed completion, fully insured</h2>
  <p>
    A concrete pour takes the driveway out of use, and an open form is not
    something anyone wants sitting for a fortnight. Every CPF job carries a
    <strong>guaranteed completion date</strong> and <strong>full insurance
    coverage</strong>, which is the part homeowners actually worry about.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>
